# Emerge and Solve on the screen

## Overview

[Emerge](https://hexdocs.pm/emerge) is an Elixir UI library in the style
of elm-ui: the UI is a tree of `el`, `row` and `column` built by plain
functions. It draws with Skia and OpenGL ES straight on the phone's DRM
display, using the Adreno GPU through Mesa.

[Solve](https://hexdocs.pm/solve) keeps application state outside the
UI. Controllers own the state and handle events; the view subscribes to
what they expose and rerenders when it changes.

Only one UI can own the screen at a time, so stop any Scenic viewport
from notebook 11 first.

## Free the screen

The IEx console is drawn on the screen too. Detach it while Emerge runs:

```elixir
fbcon =
  Path.wildcard("/sys/class/vtconsole/vtcon*")
  |> Enum.find(&(File.read!(Path.join(&1, "name")) =~ "frame buffer"))

File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "0")
```

## Find the display

The screen is driven by the `msm` DRM driver. Its card number depends on
probe order, so look it up:

```elixir
drm_card =
  Path.wildcard("/sys/class/drm/card?")
  |> Enum.find(fn card ->
    card |> Path.join("device/driver") |> File.read_link!() |> Path.basename() =~ "msm"
  end)
  |> then(&("/dev/dri/" <> Path.basename(&1)))

{:ok, outputs} = EmergeSkia.drm_outputs(drm_card: drm_card)
{drm_card, outputs}
```

## State with Solve

A controller owns one slice of state and the events that change it. An
app runs the controllers.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Counter do
  use Solve.Controller, events: [:increment, :decrement]

  @impl true
  def init(_params, _dependencies), do: %{count: 0}

  def increment(_payload, state), do: %{state | count: state.count + 1}
  def decrement(_payload, state), do: %{state | count: state.count - 1}
end

defmodule Demo.State do
  use Solve

  @impl Solve
  def controllers, do: [controller!(name: :counter, module: Demo.Counter)]
end

{:ok, app} = Demo.State.start_link(name: Demo.State)
```

## The view

`solve/2` reads the counter and subscribes to it. `event/2` turns a
controller event into something a button can send. When the counter
changes, `handle_solve_updated/2` rerenders.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.View do
  use Emerge
  use Solve.Lookup

  @impl Viewport
  # A notebook module belongs to no OTP application, so name the one whose
  # priv/ Emerge should load assets from.
  def mount(opts),
    do: {:ok, Keyword.merge([backend: :drm, otp_app: :nerves_livebook_fp3], opts)}

  @impl Viewport
  def render() do
    counter = solve(Demo.State, :counter)

    column(
      [
        width(fill()),
        height(fill()),
        spacing(60),
        Background.color(gradient([color(:indigo, 900), color(:sky, 800)], 180)),
        Font.color(color(:white))
      ],
      [
        el([center_x(), padding_xy(0, 200), Font.size(80)], text("Hello from Nerves!")),
        el([center_x(), Font.size(200)], text("#{counter.count}")),
        row([center_x(), spacing(60)], [
          button("-", event(counter, :decrement)),
          button("+", event(counter, :increment))
        ])
      ]
    )
  end

  defp button(label, on_press) do
    Input.button(
      [
        width(px(260)),
        height(px(260)),
        Border.rounded(130),
        Background.color(color(:slate, 800)),
        Font.size(120),
        Font.center(),
        Event.on_press(on_press)
      ],
      el([center_x(), center_y()], text(label))
    )
  end

  @impl Solve.Lookup
  def handle_solve_updated(_updated, state), do: {:ok, Viewport.rerender(state)}
end
```

## Show it

```elixir
{:ok, viewport} = Demo.View.start_link(drm_card: drm_card)
```

Tap the buttons. The count lives in `Demo.State`, not in the view, so
anything else can change it too and the screen follows:

```elixir
Solve.dispatch(Demo.State, :counter, :increment, nil)
```

## A GPU showcase

Everything below is drawn by the Adreno GPU every frame: a background
gradient that shifts colour, glowing orbs orbiting the centre, and a
spinning, pulsing card with a soft shadow. Animations are declared as
keyframes; the renderer interpolates them without calling back into
Elixir. Tap the card to add orbs.

```elixir
defmodule Demo.Showcase do
  use Emerge
  alias Emerge.UI.{Animation, Nearby, Transform}

  @hues [:sky, :fuchsia, :lime, :amber, :rose, :violet, :emerald, :cyan]

  @impl Viewport
  def mount(opts) do
    {:ok, %{orbs: 8},
     Keyword.merge(
       [backend: :drm, otp_app: :nerves_livebook_fp3, renderer_stats_log: true],
       opts
     )}
  end

  @impl Viewport
  def render(%{orbs: orbs}) do
    el(
      [width(fill()), height(fill()), background()] ++
        Enum.map(0..(orbs - 1), &Nearby.in_front(orb(&1, orbs))),
      el([center_x(), center_y()], card(orbs))
    )
  end

  # Three gradients, cycled in a loop. Keyframes need the same number of stops.
  defp background do
    Animation.animate(
      [
        [Background.color(gradient([color(:indigo, 950), color(:violet, 800), color(:sky, 900)], 160))],
        [Background.color(gradient([color(:slate, 950), color(:fuchsia, 800), color(:indigo, 900)], 200))],
        [Background.color(gradient([color(:indigo, 950), color(:violet, 800), color(:sky, 900)], 160))]
      ],
      8000,
      :ease_in_out,
      :loop
    )
  end

  # An orb on a circular orbit around the centre. Each gets its own radius,
  # speed, size and starting angle, and pulses as it goes.
  defp orb(i, count) do
    hue = Enum.at(@hues, rem(i, length(@hues)))
    size = 60 + rem(i * 37, 70)
    radius = 250 + rem(i * 53, 250)
    phase = 2 * :math.pi() * i / count
    period = 4000 + rem(i * 911, 5000)

    orbit =
      for step <- 0..24 do
        a = phase + 2 * :math.pi() * step / 24
        [Transform.move_x(radius * :math.cos(a)), Transform.move_y(radius * 1.6 * :math.sin(a))]
      end

    el(
      [
        center_x(),
        center_y(),
        Animation.animate(orbit, period, :linear, :loop)
      ],
      el(
        [
          width(px(size)),
          height(px(size)),
          Border.rounded(size / 2),
          Background.color(gradient([color(hue, 200), color(hue, 600)], 135)),
          Border.glow(color(hue, 400), size / 2),
          Animation.animate(
            [[Transform.scale(0.7)], [Transform.scale(1.2)], [Transform.scale(0.7)]],
            1500 + rem(i * 173, 1500),
            :ease_in_out,
            :loop
          )
        ],
        none()
      )
    )
  end

  defp card(orbs) do
    Input.button(
      [
        width(px(560)),
        height(px(560)),
        Border.rounded(80),
        Border.width(4),
        Border.color(color(:white)),
        Background.color(gradient([color(:fuchsia, 500), color(:sky, 400)], 45)),
        Border.shadow(offset: {0, 40}, blur: 80, color: color(:slate, 950)),
        Font.color(color(:white)),
        Event.on_press(:more),
        Animation.animate(
          [
            [Transform.rotate(-8), Transform.scale(0.95)],
            [Transform.rotate(8), Transform.scale(1.05)],
            [Transform.rotate(-8), Transform.scale(0.95)]
          ],
          3000,
          :ease_in_out,
          :loop
        )
      ],
      column([center_x(), center_y(), spacing(20)], [
        el([center_x(), Font.size(160)], text("#{orbs}")),
        el([center_x(), Font.size(48)], text("orbs · tap for more"))
      ])
    )
  end

  @impl Viewport
  def handle_info(:more, state) do
    {:noreply, Viewport.rerender(%{state | orbs: Kernel.min(state.orbs + 8, 96)})}
  end
end
```

Swap the counter for the showcase:

```elixir
GenServer.stop(viewport)
{:ok, viewport} = Demo.Showcase.start_link(drm_card: drm_card)
```

`renderer_stats_log: true` logs frame timings every five seconds. Watch
them in the phone's log (`RingLogger.next`) as you add orbs, to see how
far the GPU goes before frames slow down.

## Stop

```elixir
GenServer.stop(viewport)
GenServer.stop(app)
File.write!(Path.join(fbcon, "bind"), "1")
```
